Finding: {"title":"PATCH /parts/:id allows duplicate partNumber with no uniqueness check","severity":"LOW","area":"Data Integrity","files":["src/server/routes/routes-parts.ts"],"detail":"`PATCH /parts/:id` (lines 344-424) allows updating `partNumber` (line 383-384) with no check against existing parts in the same org/location for a duplicate part number, unlike `routes-aircraft-make-models.ts` which explicitly catches and translates a unique-constraint violation (`aircraft_make_models_unique_idx`) into a friendly 409. If there's no DB-level unique constraint on `(flightSchoolId, partNumber)` either, two catalog rows can end up with the same part number.","impact":"Duplicate part numbers in the same school's catalog would break part lookup/scan flows (`parts-scanner.tsx` ManualLookup search, QR scan resolution) — ambiguous which catalog row a physical part maps to, and could cause the wrong part's stock to be decremented on a work-order scan.","recommendation":"Add a unique index on `(flight_school_id, part_number)` (or location-scoped equivalent) in the schema, and catch/translate the constraint violation in both POST and PATCH handlers the same way aircraft-make-models.ts does.","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

